# Logging (Grafana) application secrets — template. The real file is
# .sops.env (sops-encrypted dotenv), decrypted on the host into .env by
# decrypt-sops-envs.service and read through `env_file`.
#
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   sops -e -i .sops.env
# (encryption needs only the public recipient in ../../.sops.yaml — no key,
# no sudo; `sopsedit stacks/logging/.sops.env` is the edit-in-place path)
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/logging.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# 🚨 QUOTING: a value containing `$` MUST be single-quoted. The decrypt path
# sources these, and Compose interpolates env_file values too (finding #30) —
# single-quoted passes through intact, bare and double-quoted get mangled.
# `openssl rand -base64` can emit `+` and `/` but never `$`, so the generation
# command below is safe unquoted; a hand-picked password may not be.
#
# ONLY GRAFANA HAS SECRETS. Loki runs with auth_enabled: false and is
# reachable only from inside this compose project, and Alloy authenticates to
# nothing — neither has an env_file and neither should acquire one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Grafana admin account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🚨 READ ONLY ONCE, ON FIRST START. Grafana seeds the admin user when it
# creates grafana.db and never revisits these variables. Changing them later
# does nothing visible: the container comes up healthy with the OLD password
# still in force. To actually rotate it:
#
#   docker exec -it grafana grafana-cli admin reset-admin-password '<new>'
#
# and update this file so a future restore from a wiped database agrees.
#
# ⚠ This is the SECOND login, underneath forward auth — the Windmill and
# Beszel arrangement. Authentik gates the vhost; Grafana does not read
# X-Authentik-* headers in this configuration, so a human logs in twice. That
# is deliberate: Grafana's header auth (auth.proxy) would trust whatever
# reaches it, and this stack's whole point is being able to read the logs when
# other things are broken — including, potentially, the VPS that runs the
# outpost. A local admin that works with the tailnet up and Authentik down is
# the property worth keeping.
#
#   Generate: openssl rand -base64 30
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=changeme_grafana_admin_username
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_long_random_password
